  "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot t+{x}_{2}\\cdot {t}^{2}=0,\n$$\n\n \nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that all linear combinations of\n\n$$\n{t,{t}^{2}}\n$$\n\nresult in polynomials without the constant term.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, the polynomial ${t}^{2}+1$ can't be written as a linear combination of the set  ${t,{t}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
